    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Vertex of a Horizontal Parabola by Completing the Square",
    "problem": "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $3{y}^{2}-2x+18y+29=0.$",
    "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $y$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-2x+18y+29 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}+18y & =2x-29 \\\\ 3[{y}^{2}+6y] & =2x-29 \\\\ 3[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =2x-29 \\\\ 3(y+3)^{2}-27 & =2x-29 \\\\ 3(y+3)^{2} & =2x-29+27 \\\\ 3(y+3)^{2} & =2x-2 \\\\ 3(y+3)^{2} & =2(x-1) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-1) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{6})(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k)=(1,-3).$",
